use xfreerdp or rdesktop
xfreerdp /u:username /p:password /v:x.x.x.x(:port) /d:domain.com /workarea /smart-sizing /cert:ignore
xfreerdp /u:username /p:password /v:x.x.x.x +clipboard +drive:kali,/home/kali/Downloads

rdesktop
rdesktop <IP>  <== login w/out username//password to see what the OS version is.
rdesktop -u <username> <IP>
rdesktop -d <domain> -u <username> -p <password> <IP>
xfreerdp [/d:domain] /u:<username> /p:<password> /v:<IP>
xfreerdp [/d:domain] /u:<username> /pth:<hash> /v:<IP> #Pass the hash

To enable RDP through powershell
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\System\CurrentControlSet\Control\Terminal Server' -name "fDenyTSConnections" -value 0
Enable-NetFirewallRule -DisplayGroup "Remote Desktop"

Evil-winrm
when there is port 5985 open you can use evil-winrm with username and password to connect. even ntlm or hash can work. look for those in RDP or remote desktop related folders or files.

